---
import Layout from "../components/Layout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";

const crumbs = [{ name: "Home", path: "/" }, { name: "How We Calculate" }];
---

<Layout
  title="How We Calculate"
  description="How HomeDIYCalc works out material quantities — the formulas, sources, and limits behind every calculator. No ads, no tracking, no account."
  canonical="/how-we-calculate/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900 sm:text-4xl">How We Calculate</h1>
  <div class="mt-6 max-w-3xl space-y-4 text-slate-700">
    <p>
      Every HomeDIYCalc calculator runs entirely in your browser, in plain
      TypeScript. There is no backend, no database, and no server involved
      in a calculation: you type in your measurements, the formula runs on
      your device, and the result appears immediately. Nothing you enter is
      sent anywhere, and nothing is stored unless you choose to save a
      result to My Project — which also lives only in your own browser.
    </p>
    <h2 class="pt-2 text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">The formulas</h2>
    <p>
      Each calculator page shows its own formula in the “How this is
      calculated” section, with a worked example using real numbers, so you
      can check the result yourself. The formulas are plain arithmetic —
      area, volume, and unit conversion — plus the coverage and yield rates
      printed by the product manufacturers. Package quantities — bags,
      boxes, gallons, sheets — always round up from the calculated amount,
      so the calculator never recommends a fraction of a package. The
      result is still an estimate for the measurements you enter — see
      Limitations below.
    </p>
    <h2 class="pt-2 text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Where the numbers come from</h2>
    <p>
      Coverage and yield rates start from the manufacturers’ own
      published data. A few figures are stated planning factors — for
      example, the standard waste allowance on a material page — and
      real-world coverage varies by product, surface condition, and
      geometry:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
      <li>
        <strong class="text-slate-900">Concrete, concrete slab, concrete bags, mortar, brick:</strong>
        QUIKRETE Concrete Mix No. 1101 and Mortar Mix No. 1102 yield figures.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-slate-900">Paint:</strong> Sherwin-Williams interior latex
        coverage data sheets (350 ft² per gallon default).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-slate-900">Primer:</strong> Sherwin-Williams ProBlock primers,
        which print 400 ft² per gallon.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-slate-900">Tile adhesive:</strong> CUSTOM VersaBond coverage
        chart (TDS-119) per 50 lb bag, and TCNA mortar-contact requirements.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-slate-900">Waterproofing:</strong> RedGard technical data sheet
        (TDS-104) and KERDI roll coverage.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-slate-900">Drywall:</strong> USG joint-compound guidance.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-slate-900">Stairs:</strong> the International Residential Code
        (IRC) limits for a straight staircase — a maximum riser height of
        7.75 in and a minimum tread depth of 10 in.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Where a manufacturer gives a range rather than a single figure, the
      calculator uses a stated default in the middle of that range and lets
      you change it to match the product you are actually buying.
    </p>
    <h2 class="pt-2 text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">No ads. No tracking. No account.</h2>
    <p>
      The site carries no advertising, no analytics, and no tracking of
      any kind, and there is no account or login. The site does not load
      third-party scripts: its Content-Security-Policy allows no external
      script, font, image, or frame source. Cost estimates appear only when
      you enter your own local prices — if you enter no price, no cost is
      shown, because we do not invent prices.
    </p>
    <h2 class="pt-2 text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Limitations</h2>
    <p>
      Calculators are estimates, not guarantees. They work out the
      quantities for the measurements you enter; they do not account for
      every site condition — slope, compaction, spillage, damaged material,
      or local building codes. Always buy a little extra for cuts and
      mistakes, confirm quantities with your supplier, and check your local
      building code before you build — the{" "}
      <a href="/calculators/stair-calculator/" class="underline">
        Stair Calculator
      </a>{" "}
      gives preliminary planning estimates under the 2021 IRC limits stated on
      its page; it does not guarantee code compliance, and local codes can
      amend those limits. For structural work, consult a qualified
      professional. See the <a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
